data.content:
The lightest approach for modern .NET (8+) is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class — no new packages, no custom interfaces needed.

Here's the pattern:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In production** — change nothing, the default uses the real clock:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator();
```

**In tests** — inject `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (one small NuGet):
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-30)); // move time backward
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — zero production dependencies
- Optional parameter means no breaking changes to callers
- No DI container needed
- `FakeTimeProvider` is a single test-only NuGet: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`

